WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:40.149
When I was at boarding school sent away during
the war as a little boy, I had a sense of

00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:47.449
imprisonment and powerlessness and I longed
for movement and power, superhuman power and

00:00:47.449 --> 00:00:49.739
ease of movement.

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:59.149
I enjoyed these briefly, and dreams of flying
and in a different way when I went horse riding

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:01.510
in the village near school.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:09.860
I love the power and subtleness of my horse
and I can still evoked it’s easy, joyous

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:10.860
movement.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.149
It's warmth.

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:15.200
And sweet hay smell.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.690
Most of all, I love motorbikes.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:26.310
My father had one before the war a Scott Flying
Squirrel with a big water-cooled engine and

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.690
an exhaust like a scream.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:34.189
And I wanted a powerful bike too.

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:39.890
By the time I was 14, it was understood that
I was going to be a doctor.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:47.830
My mother and father were both physicians
and so were my two older brothers.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:52.800
I was not sure however, that I wanted to be
a doctor.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.030
I could no longer nourish ambitions to be
a chemist.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:03.909
Chemistry itself had advanced beyond the 18th
and 19th century in organic chemistry, I love

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:05.320
so much.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:16.629
But at 14 or 15, inspired by my school biology
teacher and by Steinbeck's Cannery Row, I

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:21.769
thought I would like to become a marine biologist.

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:25.800
When I got my scholarship to Oxford, I faced
a choice.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:33.140
Should I stick to zoology or become a pre-med
student and do anatomy, biochemistry, and

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:36.920
physiology?

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.000
It was especially the physiology of the senses
that fascinated me.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.730
How did we see color, depth, movement?

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:49.480
How did we recognize anything?

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:55.980
How did we make sense of the world?

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.110
I have developed these interests from an early
age through having visible migraines.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:08.519
For beside the brilliant zigzags, which held
attack, I might during an attack, lose the

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:09.840
sense of color.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.280
Or depth.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.720
Or movement.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.510
Or even the ability to recognize anything.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:23.140
My vision could be unmade, deconstructed in
front of me, and then be remade, reconstructed

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.950
all in the space of a few minutes.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:35.890
Incidentally I think this is why I wrote my
first book on migraine and devoted a third

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.140
of it to visual migraine.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:50.080
I knew well of what I wrote.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.410
I'm Robert Krulwich.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:56.300
I'm a reporter, cover lots of science things.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:01.040
I met Oliver because he was lying down and
I was standing up.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.083
He had broken his leg after having missed
his leg for a long period.

00:04:05.083 --> 00:04:06.760
The guy was like a mess.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.810
He had lost his leg to his brain.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.680
He'd found his leg and then broken it again
and now he was lying in a room in Einstein

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.329
Medical Center in the Bronx.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:21.199
And a friend of mine said, I was working at
the time for All Things Considered for NPR.

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:28.979
So there's this older guy up in the Bronx
who's like the best storyteller ever.

00:04:28.979 --> 00:04:35.110
He had- had, I find it strikingly hard to
speak in the past tense, and he had a talent

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.849
for beauty that I've rarely run into.

00:04:38.849 --> 00:04:42.449
There is beauty all over in the world, even
in the most troubled places.

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:48.830
But it takes a special kind of person to see
the beauty here and there and there and to

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:54.270
write it down and then somehow, in translation,
it becomes more beautiful.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.059
And that, that's the essence of it.

00:04:57.059 --> 00:05:07.719
For me, is that he took- he found the beauty
and then rendered it even more beautiful.

00:05:07.719 --> 00:05:13.440
The listening act was an empathic act almost
always.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:19.009
If you just parade the people that he chose
to look at, people who are autistic, people

00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:23.599
who are colorblind, people who couldn't hear,
people who had lost their sight, gained their

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:26.199
sight and lost it again.

00:05:26.199 --> 00:05:33.830
People who were Touretters, people who were
hallucinating all the time.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:40.629
You parade these people and then you add Oliver
and all Oliver does is he makes them so real

00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:47.110
and so familiar that you lose the sense of
them being other.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:54.800
And then, if a Harold Pinter or a Robin Williams
or another great artist reads these accounts

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:01.020
and decides to make a movie or a play about
what Oliver has written about, then more people

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:07.559
hear about these folks and the narrative just
moves out from Oliver into the world and the

00:06:07.559 --> 00:06:15.710
end result is, and this is rare to say about
anyone, any doctor, any artist, that the world

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:24.229
gains new friends through the act of intimately
sitting with these people and making them

00:06:24.229 --> 00:06:25.229
your friend.

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:28.460
Then the friendship expands because poets
do it.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:29.509
Playwrights do it.

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:33.580
Then all of a sudden everybody can do it.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:38.879
My name is Robert Klitzman and I'm a professor
of psychiatry here at Columbia University.

00:06:38.879 --> 00:06:44.520
I, at one point here in New York, saw Oliver
at a party and introduced myself and we talked

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.599
and I mentioned I'd just written a book called
In a House of Dreams and Glass becoming a

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:52.460
psychiatrist and Oliver said, "I'd love to
see it."

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:54.889
I sent him a copy of the book thinking, well
that's very nice.

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:57.879
He's a big name, I'll never hear from him,
but that's okay.

00:06:57.879 --> 00:07:04.719
And then I received a long handwritten letter
from him in- looked like blue crayon.

00:07:04.719 --> 00:07:10.809
And he said he loved the book and said that,
"Your writing is quote both 'light and deep

00:07:10.809 --> 00:07:12.599
at the same time.'"

00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:16.090
Well Oliver affected my work and my life in
several ways.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:22.969
Perhaps the most important is that he created
a precedent for things that I and others have

00:07:22.969 --> 00:07:28.879
then later done that was much easier to do
because Oliver had paved the way.

00:07:28.879 --> 00:07:34.639
It's one of the works I read was Oliver's
work, A Leg To Stand On, which came out before

00:07:34.639 --> 00:07:36.949
The Man Who Mistook His Wife For A Hat.

00:07:36.949 --> 00:07:40.680
It's not one of the books that Oliver is most
known for.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.889
It's probably his least known book or one
of his two or three least known books, but

00:07:44.889 --> 00:07:48.110
was about his own experience as a patient.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:52.530
And I always felt that that was important
for Oliver's development.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:59.900
So it's not just that he had a gift for use
of words, for use of language, for being able

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:07.619
to tell the story of someone's life, of someone
dealing with whatever kind of neurologic problem,

00:08:07.619 --> 00:08:10.689
for lack of a better term, they may have.

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:17.589
If it were a patient for instance, but he
had this wonderful sensibility to look at

00:08:17.589 --> 00:08:23.679
not just the brain and the neurology of it,
but the anthropology of it, the whole human-ness

00:08:23.679 --> 00:08:27.419
of it.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:30.969
I'm Brian Greene.

00:08:30.969 --> 00:08:37.479
Here in the flesh.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:43.450
I first met Oliver Sacks about 17 years ago.

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:49.490
It was when my first book, The Elegant Universe,
had just come out and I was invited to what

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:51.920
you might call my first literary gathering.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.760
So there were a lot of writers in the room
at this dinner.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:02.230
I recognized a lot of science writers, books
that I had read, but then I spotted Oliver

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.550
across the room.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:12.330
And it was he who I really wanted to meet
because, look I have and had great respect

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:13.880
for science writers.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.820
That's a very important thing to do.

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:23.640
But to write about the afflicted, those who
society for so long has kept under the radar,

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:30.350
those who can make us uncomfortable and to
find the beauty, and the humanity, and the

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:37.260
grace in their stories and make us care about
these individuals, want to know about these

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:44.190
individuals, feel compelled to read about
these individuals, and allow us to catch our

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:52.390
breath at the surprising, sometimes debilitating,
wondrous, poetic qualities that can emerge

00:09:52.390 --> 00:10:00.720
from but a small variation in the same handful
of grey matter that we all have inside our

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.870
heads.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:06.050
That wasn't science writing.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:10.620
That was a ferocious talent and a rare gift.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:20.240
So I'm in this room sort of in this awestruck,
starstruck state and Oliver, over cocktails,

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:27.380
turned to me and said that that very day,
he'd finished reading my book.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.460
And I kept my composure.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:33.520
But inside, I was like, "Shit that's amazing,
right?

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:38.910
And then he goes on and said how he was so
thrilled to learn about what's happening at

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:40.550
the cutting edge of unified theory.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.490
Again, I was like wow this is amazing.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:48.180
And then he said, but the book itself is a
little slippery.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:53.990
And I was like whoa, you know, so I was about
to kind of launch into, "Well you know, even

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:57.900
we professional physicists, we find quantum
mechanics slippery too, you know."

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:58.900
And he said, "No, no.

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:02.860
Brian, Brian, what I mean is the book is literally
slippery.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:08.110
I- I was reading it in the tub and the hardcover
just slipped right out of the jacket into-

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:12.580
and he says, "I'm- I'm probably the first
person to have already bought two copies of

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.300
your book."

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:15.620
No.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:20.800
So the next the- next time we crossed paths,
Oliver and I crossed paths.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:29.260
It was actually in May of 2008 at the first
World Science Festival.

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:35.090
Because as -as Tracy said, Oliver was a great
friend of the festival.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:42.310
You know he, year after year would sit in
a chair just like this, oftentimes on this

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:44.240
very stage.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:51.370
But the encounter that really sticks in my
mind did happen at that first inaugural festival.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:53.990
And it wasn't an event that took place here.

00:11:53.990 --> 00:12:00.320
It was an event that took place at the Abyssinian
Baptist Church on 138th street.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:03.010
And was anyone at that event?

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.010
Anyone here?

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.180
Two people, that's very good.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:07.500
Yes excellent.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:12.720
There were actually 2000 people at this event,
up there in 2008.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:21.060
I mean Tracy Day and I were absolutely intent
on creating different kinds of science programming,

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:26.610
putting together unusual pieces to create
an interesting experience and we spoke to

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:31.240
Oliver about this and he got very excited
about one idea we had, which was to pair him

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:34.540
with the Abyssinian Baptist Church Choir.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:38.320
And do a program on music and the brain.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:44.670
So we're up there at the church, it's filling
in.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.820
The people were just squeezing into the pews.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:57.430
Every seat was taken and I'm about to go up
on stage to introduce the program.

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:04.800
And Oliver turns to me and he says, "Two Jewish
boys.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:11.080
About to give a tag team sermon on science
at a Baptist church."

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:20.110
He says, "You know, the Lord does work in
mysterious ways."

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:32.380
Oliver Sacks, you've written somewhat about
the fact that the increments of things we

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:35.290
see in the moments of time could change.

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:37.880
The speed of them could change.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:41.000
I mean, I don't quite understand that.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:44.760
Well, theoretically anyway.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.370
Well certainly.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:51.890
Well William James thought they could change
theoretically, depending on how many events

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:55.340
one could perceive in a second.

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:56.340
How many events.

00:13:56.340 --> 00:14:00.340
By that you mean anything at all, like I see
that spotlight there's whatever, it may and

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:03.490
doesn't have to be a theater or a concert,
just anything.

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.460
Could be flashes of light.

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:13.080
In- H.G. Wells deals with this in a fictional
way in the time machine and in stories he

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:19.400
writes where he imagines that people can be
accelerated and then they will move with great

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:24.010
speed through the world and the world will
seem to be almost frozen or in slow motion

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:25.900
and vice versa.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:30.580
And I've seen something similar I think in
some of my patients, who may overlap with-

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:34.330
with yours.

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:44.830
In particular, people with Tourette's Syndrome
who make sudden movements and sometimes sudden

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:48.400
noises can be very accelerated.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:52.440
So accelerated, they can easily catch a fly
in midair.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:53.440
Can they really do that?

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:54.810
They can really do it.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:59.100
And if you ask them, they don't feel that
they are very fast.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:03.340
They feel the flies are rather slow.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.810
And so with the rest of us.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:10.920
And again,
Wait a moment, so they're fast but the time

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:14.020
is the same, but they're just speedier.

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:19.590
They do more and experience more in a particular
time.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:25.600
William James wrote, in an allied context,
he said monkeys these people seem to us, whilst

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:28.310
we seem to them reptilian.

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:37.120
Now the term reptilian, while the unpleasant
word, is sometimes used for the relative lack

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:43.100
or slowness of facial expression and of movement
generally in people with Parkinsonism.

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:48.060
And Parkinsonism, is almost physiologically,
the opposite of Tourettes.

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:53.750
Both of these depend on what one has been
looking at, the amount of a transmitter called

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:55.340
dopamine in the brain.

00:15:55.340 --> 00:16:01.540
So people with Parkinson's- Parkinsonism may
move very slowly and feel that the rest of

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:06.690
us, and the clocks are going while the fast.

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:18.730
I'm Jay Neugeboren and I met Oliver in 1996
when I had written a book about me and my

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:19.730
brother.

00:16:19.730 --> 00:16:21.900
My brother had been a mental patient most
of his adult life.

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:28.960
And I thought it would be a book Oliver would
find of interest if I could get it to him.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:34.980
I didn't know him, so I sent it to him care
of the Bronx Psychiatric Center.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:40.470
And a few weeks later, I got a wonderful letter
back from him that he'd read my book, said

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:47.810
some very kind things about it, and then he
said, I too have a brother like your brother,

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:49.200
Robert.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.040
And he's been through a lot of the things
your brother has been through.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:56.250
I'm wondering if I should show him your book.

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.500
And that's- he gave me his phone number.

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:04.970
We talked and we became- we've been friends
ever since.

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:13.159
I loved the fact that from the very first
letter he wrote me about the book I'd written,

00:17:13.159 --> 00:17:18.370
about me and my brother, imagining Robert,
that he didn't send an email.

00:17:18.370 --> 00:17:20.309
I never had an email from him at all.

00:17:20.309 --> 00:17:23.670
Twenty years we knew another.

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:27.120
But he wrote letters and he wrote them by
hand.

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:28.620
They weren't typed.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:31.049
They would have editing within it.

00:17:31.049 --> 00:17:35.210
There'd be words crossed out and he'd put
a different word on top of it.

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:41.000
Clearly, he re-read them, wanted to make sure
that he said what he wanted.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:49.320
Oliver had a very large effect on the way
I saw my brother, the way I saw myself, where

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:57.409
I saw myself in relation to my brother, and
I believe he had a great effect on anyone

00:17:57.409 --> 00:18:02.919
who read him, because he wrote about people
who were in effect, "the other."

00:18:02.919 --> 00:18:10.460
He wrote about people who are not like you
and me.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:20.990
I think Oliver's great distinction was his
ability to join the sciences to the humanities,

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:28.549
per se, that is to say not that they are different,
the sciences are part of the humanities.

00:18:28.549 --> 00:18:40.360
But to be able as a writer, to make us understand
how wondrous and complicated the workings

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:42.830
of any human mind are.

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:52.299
The meanest of us has something here that
is more complicated than anything in the known

00:18:52.299 --> 00:18:54.139
universe.

00:18:54.139 --> 00:19:04.419
And he enabled us to understand the ways that
difference and individuality could show themselves

00:19:04.419 --> 00:19:05.419
forth.

00:19:05.419 --> 00:19:09.120
I'm Dr. Mark Homonoff.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.470
I first saw Oliver when I was a medical student.

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:18.470
This was in the period 1970 to 1974 at Albert
Einstein College of Medicine up in the Bronx

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:22.840
and it was pointed out that he was an eccentric
member of the faculty.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:23.840
He rode a motorcycle.

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.289
He wore a leather jacket.

00:19:25.289 --> 00:19:28.779
He rode a motorcycle to rounds.

00:19:28.779 --> 00:19:33.909
But it was in an era of eccentricity where
amongst other things, psychiatric clinics

00:19:33.909 --> 00:19:36.890
were set up in pool halls in the South Bronx.

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:41.410
And he was only contemplating some mode of
expression for himself.

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:42.889
But it hadn't come about.

00:19:42.889 --> 00:19:49.080
He would submit many of these articles to
major American medical journals and they would

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:54.840
just be rejected as being too journalistic,
too descriptive.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:58.559
Some of the some of the review pieces he got
packed said we don't agree with your statistics.

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:01.309
There were no statistics of what he did.

00:20:01.309 --> 00:20:07.779
It- it was his discussion, and his perception
of the patient, and the world of the patient

00:20:07.779 --> 00:20:12.210
and how the patient functioned within the
realm of disease he suffered from.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:19.549
Oliver was able to take in almost any kind
of information and for Oliver, extraneous

00:20:19.549 --> 00:20:23.049
information, what most physicians would consider
to be extraneous information.

00:20:23.049 --> 00:20:28.700
And for anyone we consider to be extraneous
information in any number of capacities in

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:34.830
which Oliver worked, operated, was really
just fodder for his imagination.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:43.879
He could see the heroism in patients overcoming
their illness, in ways in which patients worked

00:20:43.879 --> 00:20:49.590
around problems, whether it was a patient's
obsessive thoughts, or whether it was difficulty

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:53.320
with gait, or whether it was difficulty with
movement.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:21:00.280
How they could fit this into their world and
lead—lead- lead a creative and productive

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:03.850
life despite what other impediments they ran
across.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:05.640
And he attempted to assist them.

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:12.659
And he tried to assist them in that process
and in many cases did.

00:21:12.659 --> 00:21:15.289
My name is John Hockenberry.

00:21:15.289 --> 00:21:23.070
I've been an acquaintance of Oliver Sacks
exclusively through the domain of journalism.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:28.350
But we were friends, because I have a disability.

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:34.350
And that's because his particular curiosity
about the details of my life made him instantly

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:38.929
interested in the actually interesting things
about me.

00:21:38.929 --> 00:21:45.100
We were friends from the first moment we met.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:52.690
Oliver Sacks talks, as you've heard tonight,
about flying, about being freed, unburdened,

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:56.410
being able to fly.

00:21:56.410 --> 00:22:04.070
And indeed, it is the case that Oliver Sacks
could go places that normal humans could not

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:05.100
go.

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:10.369
And here's an example from a moment at the
World Science Festival in front of a live

00:22:10.369 --> 00:22:19.230
audience a few years ago.

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:25.019
I mean you- you have had many hallucinatory
experiences, some of which were an accidental

00:22:25.019 --> 00:22:31.540
consequence of substances that you were working
with, and others more deliberate.

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:35.919
Well there are many different motives.

00:22:35.919 --> 00:22:43.299
And it was and it was all a long time ago.

00:22:43.299 --> 00:22:46.090
Was all between '63 and '67.

00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:49.110
But- but certainly one of them.

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:53.170
I'd-I'd been puzzling about indigo.

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:59.499
Indigo was inserted into the spectrum by Newton
so was orange.

00:22:59.499 --> 00:23:04.480
He felt there should be seven colors, as there's
seven notes in the musical scale.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:09.869
But it seemed to me that no two people agreed
as to what indigo was like.

00:23:09.869 --> 00:23:17.299
And it was in fact felt that the pigment derived
from the indigo plant was not really indigo.

00:23:17.299 --> 00:23:22.539
And I decided I wanted to see indigo.

00:23:22.539 --> 00:23:26.269
I couldn't imagine it, but I thought I might
be able to hallucinate it.

00:23:26.269 --> 00:23:30.730
So I built a sort of pharmacological launch
pad.

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:38.070
A sort of-
That- that's different from controlled substance,

00:23:38.070 --> 00:23:39.070
ok?

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:40.869
Pharmacological launch pad.

00:23:40.869 --> 00:23:41.889
I love that.

00:23:41.889 --> 00:23:44.850
As a writer here, it's what we got.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:54.460
So a base of amphetamine and then some LSD
and a little a little cannabis on top.

00:23:54.460 --> 00:24:05.559
So I'll take a shot of amphetamine with a
LSD chaser and a spot of cannabis.

00:24:05.559 --> 00:24:11.970
And that gets you-
Well, it got me fairly pickled.

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:15.870
I think stoned is the right word anyhow.

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:22.350
When- when I felt ready, I said I want to
see indigo now.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:34.669
And as if thrown by a paint brush, a trembling
pear shaped blob of the most wonderful color

00:24:34.669 --> 00:24:38.090
appeared on the wall.

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:39.090
Indigo.

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:47.429
And it not be seemed wonderfully luminous,
but it filled me with a sort of mystical,

00:24:47.429 --> 00:24:48.600
almost religious joy.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:57.799
I felt it was numinous as well as luminous
and although I'm an old Jewish atheist, I

00:24:57.799 --> 00:25:09.409
thought this is the color of heaven.

00:25:09.409 --> 00:25:17.700
So as you can see, Oliver Sacks had a scientist's
"I'll try anything once" spirit of adventure

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:23.590
and "I'll try some things a few times" spirit
of mischief all rolled into one.

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:29.409
Like most people, I first met Oliver Sacks
in the pages of his many wonderful books.

00:25:29.409 --> 00:25:33.470
There his storytelling voice seemed so unlike
that of other writers.

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:39.179
This methodical and reverential voice could
explain the miracles of the brain by careful

00:25:39.179 --> 00:25:41.919
observations of its deficits.

00:25:41.919 --> 00:25:47.309
I could gasp and sigh to hear his stories
of what the brain could still do, even when

00:25:47.309 --> 00:25:49.289
it couldn't.

00:25:49.289 --> 00:25:55.230
His was a voice clear and exceptional, like
a French horn soloing above an orchestra,

00:25:55.230 --> 00:26:00.650
deliberate and beautiful complex and full
of warmth.

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:05.820
But the actual voice of Dr. Oliver Sacks was
almost a whisper.

00:26:05.820 --> 00:26:11.320
Deliberate and clear, this softer voice conveyed
not only his powers of observations, but also

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:18.159
his empathy for his patients and one of the
most eloquent writers to ever speak of the

00:26:18.159 --> 00:26:24.499
brain in person would say, blain.

00:26:24.499 --> 00:26:33.350
Almost an impediment, spoken like a child,
but brilliant in its own way.

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:37.529
After his hearing loss became more pronounced,
the whisper became deeper, sometimes with

00:26:37.529 --> 00:26:38.649
the touch of impatience.

00:26:38.649 --> 00:26:42.960
He wanted us all to listen closely and not
just to him.

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:47.530
In this moment of change in science and medicine
he was saying, "Don't miss this.

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:48.530
Listen up."

00:26:48.530 --> 00:26:50.330
He most of all celebrated his patients.

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:56.269
He constantly wondered what it would feel
like to be them, to feel their incompleteness,

00:26:56.269 --> 00:27:01.450
and to feel what it might be like to have
their brain function restored was a dream

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:03.480
of Oliver Sacks.

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:07.519
It was another dream of seeing indigo I suppose.

00:27:07.519 --> 00:27:13.450
When he became a patient, he showed us all
how the journey to death need not diminish

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:16.149
our curiosity.

00:27:16.149 --> 00:27:18.690
One time in an event in the streets, the skies
opened up.

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:21.840
Oliver and I were on our way to some panel
discussion and while I paid most attention

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:28.149
to the roads and puddles in front of my wheelchair,
Oliver was silently staring at my necktie.

00:27:28.149 --> 00:27:33.799
Eventually he said, "How did you know that
I love octopus?

00:27:33.799 --> 00:27:35.789
Your tie is full of octopus."

00:27:35.789 --> 00:27:44.490
I noticed for the very first time that indeed,
my tie was full of little line drawn cephalopods.

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:47.019
I said, "Happy to oblige."

00:27:47.019 --> 00:27:50.669
Being with Oliver was to experience moments
like this, always.

00:27:50.669 --> 00:27:54.230
He would discover something in front of your
nose.

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:59.600
I invited him to a conference 10 years ago
at MIT, all about technology and disability.

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:02.340
It was a wonderful, wonderful event.

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.100
Presenters were all disabled.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:09.520
Amputees, brain injured scientists working
to make their limbs and brains work better,

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:14.580
double leg amputee actress and model Aimee
Mullins brought a whole crate of different

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:16.700
legs that she was showing off.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:17.820
Oliver loved that.

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:22.320
A young man with cerebral palsy played his
own music that he'd composed.

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:26.669
Oliver couldn't have been happy- happier to
have been a part of it.

00:28:26.669 --> 00:28:32.440
He was for me at the end, that he felt like
one of the gang.

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:36.519
I've always believed he said that patients
always do the best research, you know.

00:28:36.519 --> 00:28:40.379
The trick is getting them to show it to you.

00:28:40.379 --> 00:28:41.679
Oliver Sacks.

00:28:41.679 --> 00:28:44.119
Doctor, patient.

00:28:44.119 --> 00:28:50.200
Did he see a difference?

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:53.850
I'm Isabelle Rapin.

00:28:53.850 --> 00:29:00.779
I'm a child neurologist retired from Albert
Einstein College of Medicine.

00:29:00.779 --> 00:29:12.590
I met Oliver Sacks probably the first month
that he came to Einstein in the fall of 1965.

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:14.710
And we've been friends ever since.

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:25.119
I think that's it was his interest in the
individual patient and the concerned about

00:29:25.119 --> 00:29:33.059
the individual consequences of neurologic
illness rather than an neurologic illness

00:29:33.059 --> 00:29:42.519
per se that led him into the direction that
his career took and into his writings, which

00:29:42.519 --> 00:29:50.070
of course, have turned up because of his talent
have turned out to be so dramatic.

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:56.779
Oliver was very helpful to patients with Parkinsonism.

00:29:56.779 --> 00:30:02.799
He made observations that no neurologist had
ever seen.

00:30:02.799 --> 00:30:11.049
He observed, when he gave it to his Post-encephalitic
Parkinsonism patients, that they developed.

00:30:11.049 --> 00:30:22.460
Yes, some of them woke up after years of being
barely communicative, frozen in place, suddenly

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:24.830
were able to move and become lively again.

00:30:24.830 --> 00:30:26.019
Does he ever speak to you?

00:30:26.019 --> 00:30:27.019
Of course not.

00:30:27.019 --> 00:30:28.019
Not in words.

00:30:28.019 --> 00:30:29.019
No change for the 91145.

00:30:29.019 --> 00:30:31.230
Your patients, doctor, haven't moved in decades.

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:38.299
What I believe, what I know, is these people
are alive inside.

00:30:38.299 --> 00:30:42.220
How do you know that doctor?

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:44.549
I know it.

00:30:44.549 --> 00:30:55.489
With 200 milligrams, he showed no response.

00:30:55.489 --> 00:31:01.960
Maybe he needs more.

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:08.429
Maybe he needs less.

00:31:08.429 --> 00:31:15.860
Oliver went from a person than people sort
of looked and didn't take too seriously, as

00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:23.559
to what neurology ended up realizing was the
best ambassador it ever had.

00:31:23.559 --> 00:31:26.549
My name is Tobias Picker.

00:31:26.549 --> 00:31:33.989
I met Oliver for the first time in my own
apartment, actually.

00:31:33.989 --> 00:31:43.749
I gave a dinner party for Oliver, which was
arranged for him by a mutual friend of ours,

00:31:43.749 --> 00:31:45.909
Ed Weinberg's.

00:31:45.909 --> 00:31:52.759
And that was about 25 years ago or so.

00:31:52.759 --> 00:32:03.190
And I was interested in meeting Oliver because
I had been diagnosed with Tourette's syndrome

00:32:03.190 --> 00:32:04.859
very late.

00:32:04.859 --> 00:32:11.830
I guess I was in my mid 30s.

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:20.989
So because at that- when I was a child when
the symptoms started, there was there was

00:32:20.989 --> 00:32:26.690
no way it could be diagnosed so it was swept
under the rug.

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:35.239
And also- the big elephant in the room through
my whole life until I became aware of Oliver's

00:32:35.239 --> 00:32:38.239
writings about it.

00:32:38.239 --> 00:32:48.919
So as we moved in similar circles, he knew
writers that I knew as a composer.

00:32:48.919 --> 00:32:54.419
I wanted to talk to him so.

00:32:54.419 --> 00:32:56.789
He came to dinner.

00:32:56.789 --> 00:33:04.399
He was very helpful to me, because he could
talk about what he what- he was observing

00:33:04.399 --> 00:33:15.599
in in a way that that made me understand how
I was being perceived by the by the outside

00:33:15.599 --> 00:33:31.099
world in a way that reassured me that I am-
wasn't- that I didn't have to be ashamed of-

00:33:31.099 --> 00:33:45.679
of having this- this thing as- as I had been
as a child growing up with it It-I think-I

00:33:45.679 --> 00:33:55.289
think Oliver said months, not to me, but I
read this about a quote of his, "The question

00:33:55.289 --> 00:33:59.749
is not what disease does the person have.

00:33:59.749 --> 00:34:04.499
The question is what person does the disease
have."

00:34:04.499 --> 00:34:12.109
I had a very difficult childhood and stayed
with me.

00:34:12.109 --> 00:34:17.800
It stayed with me for
a- well, it will stay with me forever.

00:34:17.800 --> 00:34:35.670
But knowing Oliver being able to-to be- to
spend time with Oliver and to be just to be

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:44.030
in the presence of his greatness made me feel
better about myself.

00:34:44.030 --> 00:34:57.920
In rereading the book, it dawned on me that
this this material is- is- is a terrific-

00:34:57.920 --> 00:35:06.590
terrific inspiration for dance, because it's
all it can be all that can be read as a- as

00:35:06.590 --> 00:36:27.630
a study in movement and the absence of movement.

00:36:27.630 --> 00:39:49.320
I try and 

00:39:49.320 --> 00:39:50.850
live with my patients in a way.

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:55.970
I lived next door to the hospital at that
time, and sort of share their experiences

00:39:55.970 --> 00:39:58.050
and joys and griefs.

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:02.720
But it was it was horrible to see Leonard
lose what he had.

00:40:02.720 --> 00:40:08.410
And his- his desperation and his hopelessness.

00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:11.480
And I -I felt guilty in a sense.

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:14.770
I thought, could I have known that this would
happen?

00:40:14.770 --> 00:40:17.860
Should- should one have animated him, have
awakened him in the first place?

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:22.540
And your answer was?

00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:26.490
My answer, or the patient's answer, in general
was yes.

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:32.600
And they would go ahead and say further, if
only this had been available 20 years ago

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:39.250
before we'd lost so much of life and lost
and had so much damage on our brains.

00:40:39.250 --> 00:40:45.370
And indeed, had this occurred 20 years ago,
the things wouldn't have been so tantalizing.

00:40:45.370 --> 00:40:52.360
People with ordinary Parkinson's disease get-
get a far longer good period on the drug.

00:40:52.360 --> 00:40:54.120
I'm Daniel Frank.

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:57.740
I'm the editorial director of Pantheon Books.

00:40:57.740 --> 00:41:02.710
The way Oliver worked was unique and yes,
it was completely different than anything

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:03.980
I'd seen before.

00:41:03.980 --> 00:41:09.870
The other aspect of Oliver was that, not only
would there be the pages posted on a bulletin

00:41:09.870 --> 00:41:15.580
board, there would be a chalkboard or a magic
marker board with all the possible subjects

00:41:15.580 --> 00:41:23.640
or topics, possible ordering, possible organization,
and even when I saw those had already been

00:41:23.640 --> 00:41:28.530
revised two or three times and it was a matter
of like, where are we now.

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:34.140
And so the conversation, I always felt like
when I would arrive there, this is what we're

00:41:34.140 --> 00:41:35.890
thinking now.

00:41:35.890 --> 00:41:36.930
And so I quickly learned.

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:37.930
All right.

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:38.930
This is now.

00:41:38.930 --> 00:41:41.080
It could be different by the end of our visit.

00:41:41.080 --> 00:41:43.170
It could be different tomorrow.

00:41:43.170 --> 00:41:50.770
But it was always, for me, was always this
chance to hear Oliver thinking out loud about

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:54.260
a subject, about how he was moving through
a book.

00:41:54.260 --> 00:41:57.950
What made that subject work for him.

00:41:57.950 --> 00:42:02.990
And I mean what you discovered in editing
is that there isn't necessarily a right or

00:42:02.990 --> 00:42:04.160
wrong way.

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:12.020
There is a way of telling a story that's compelling
and that's what you wanted to do each time.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:15.360
It's sort of fun if you're in the creative
business.

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:19.150
It's fun to play with fast horses.

00:42:19.150 --> 00:42:23.930
Maybe it will be one thing if you were good
at something and you found people who are

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:28.070
as good as you or less good as you and you
mentor them or whatever.

00:42:28.070 --> 00:42:30.470
And that would be like clippidy klop, clippidy
klop.

00:42:30.470 --> 00:42:36.830
But if you find friends who are better than
you, and Oliver was smarter.

00:42:36.830 --> 00:42:39.460
I mean, really smart.

00:42:39.460 --> 00:42:41.600
Just almost effortless.

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:47.990
He could consume all kinds of information
just very- very easily and he remembered it,

00:42:47.990 --> 00:42:52.750
and then he could weigh it, and measure it,
and balance it, and he could listen, and he

00:42:52.750 --> 00:43:00.070
could write, and he had a sense- that he had
crazy eyes for seeing, and have pretty big

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:01.070
heart.

00:43:01.070 --> 00:43:07.610
And I think when you when you pile up with
somebody who's so much better than you are

00:43:07.610 --> 00:43:13.140
in every category and you kind of know that
you'll never be-You could never get there.

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:15.970
It makes you as good as you can possibly be.

00:43:15.970 --> 00:43:23.920
And so the cool part for me was getting to
keep company with the fastest.

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:26.770
One of the fastest horses I'd ever met.

00:43:26.770 --> 00:43:29.850
I'm addicted to patients.

00:43:29.850 --> 00:43:31.770
I can't do without them.

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:37.720
I need to have the feeling of these other
lives, which are, which become a part of my

00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:38.720
own.

00:43:38.720 --> 00:43:39.720
Empathy isn't enough.

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:57.990
I wish I could be in their shoes or know more
exactly what it's like.

00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:01.790
There's tremendous emotion in all your animal
paintings and sculptures too.

00:44:01.790 --> 00:44:14.150
It's a sort of energized state in which there
is a sort of convulsive energy which especially

00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:21.500
takes the form of excessive movement, of sudden
movement, of compulsive tics of touching,

00:44:21.500 --> 00:44:25.410
of compulsive noises, sometimes of compulsive
thoughts.

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:32.260
I think with Shane, I have never seen on the
one hand, such severity and maybe intensity

00:44:32.260 --> 00:44:39.221
of Tourette's, but with such with such a sort
of- of- of living and accepting and sort of

00:44:39.221 --> 00:44:40.650
using it.

00:44:40.650 --> 00:44:43.770
I mean I think it's sort of inspiring to see
him.

00:44:43.770 --> 00:44:47.320
You feel your Tourette's sometimes bullies
or uses you.

00:44:47.320 --> 00:44:50.870
Do you feel you have ways of using it?

00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:55.620
I think in my art, my sculptures, my paintings.

00:44:55.620 --> 00:44:57.820
In which sort of ways?

00:44:57.820 --> 00:45:00.750
You know I can make.

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:07.230
I don't know because I- I- I -I like because-
because I know I like all the things that

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:09.500
I can I can feed that our unleash it.

00:45:09.500 --> 00:45:11.040
Unleash it is the word.

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:17.560
I know I know that when I found sculpture,
I found I found that I was hurt.

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:19.070
That was my- my world.

00:45:19.070 --> 00:45:22.020
I felt at home.

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:23.330
My name is Maria Popova.

00:45:23.330 --> 00:45:30.370
I read and I write and I write about what
I read on a website called Brain Pickings.

00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:34.360
I came to Dr. Sacks' work fairly late.

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:36.980
Late both in my life and late in his career.

00:45:36.980 --> 00:45:41.740
I grew up very far away and his books didn't
make it to the other side of the iron curtain

00:45:41.740 --> 00:45:43.720
where I grew up.

00:45:43.720 --> 00:45:48.170
And I found them as an adult and I just fell
in love with the way that he sees the world

00:45:48.170 --> 00:45:54.250
and he's able to articulate what he sees well
beyond science.

00:45:54.250 --> 00:46:00.290
I think Dr. Sacks figured himself out in the
act of writing, and there was something contagious

00:46:00.290 --> 00:46:06.670
about that, there was kind of intense curiosity
with which he examined what was on his mind

00:46:06.670 --> 00:46:08.970
and the nature of the mind.

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:14.310
All at once and pulled you into that so that
you would then examine your own mind and its

00:46:14.310 --> 00:46:16.710
nature in the act of reading.

00:46:16.710 --> 00:46:24.760
You know, it's interesting to consider how
he saw people, the people that he worked with,

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:31.850
the so-called patients in a way that's increasingly
countered to how they're seen by the medical

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:37.540
and scientific culture of today where patients
are very often reduced to data points and

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:43.710
yet here he was, telling us that the interior
lives of people matter and that they are singular

00:46:43.710 --> 00:46:49.651
and dignified indifferent and the humanity
that sprang from each interaction that he

00:46:49.651 --> 00:46:54.510
had with a person, not just a patient.

00:46:54.510 --> 00:47:01.710
That's something that is so important to preserve
and he's kind of the patron saint of this

00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:10.370
way of treating human beings.

00:47:10.370 --> 00:47:17.940
My name is Steve SILBERMAN and I met Oliver
originally because Kate Edgar, his assistant,

00:47:17.940 --> 00:47:22.540
got in touch with me to tell me that Oliver
had really enjoyed an article that I wrote

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:28.100
called The Geek Syndrome in Wired magazine,
which was one of the first articles to look

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:33.530
at autism in high tech communities like Silicon
Valley.

00:47:33.530 --> 00:47:40.610
What made Oliver’s work on autism so important
was that for decades, autistic people had

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:47.700
been identified as not having the essential
qualities that make us human.

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:55.310
So they were defined by clinicians and psychiatrists,
as for instance, lacking empathy or lacking

00:47:55.310 --> 00:48:01.200
the capacity for humor, or lacking the capacity
to love their own parents.

00:48:01.200 --> 00:48:03.990
And all of that is wrong.

00:48:03.990 --> 00:48:16.580
His portrait of Temple Grandin, An Anthropologist
on Mars, was the most humane and deep portrait

00:48:16.580 --> 00:48:20.760
of an autistic adult that had ever been written
in history.

00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:27.520
And believe me, I read all the stuff that
came before it and so Oliver simply by being

00:48:27.520 --> 00:48:35.670
open to being surprised by Temple, was able
to pick up on her humanity in such a way that

00:48:35.670 --> 00:48:38.720
he could render this fully human portrait.

00:48:38.720 --> 00:48:39.930
My name is Temple Grandin.

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:42.830
I am a Professor of Animal Science at Colorado
State University.

00:48:42.830 --> 00:48:50.230
Now of course, I have known about Oliver Sacks
his work long before I ever met him.

00:48:50.230 --> 00:48:55.340
And then one day, I got a call that he wanted
to come out and talk to me about autism.

00:48:55.340 --> 00:49:03.120
He just wanted to research some things about
autism with me and I set it up and he came

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:04.120
out.

00:49:04.120 --> 00:49:10.010
And it was very fun talking with Oliver, and
one of the things I remember the most is he

00:49:10.010 --> 00:49:14.740
wanted to go up to the National Park and I
had to stop him from jumping in the river

00:49:14.740 --> 00:49:18.340
and said, "Oliver, you're going to go over
a dam if you jump in that river.

00:49:18.340 --> 00:49:22.060
No, you absolutely cannot go with that river."

00:49:22.060 --> 00:49:25.300
And I stopped him from doing that.

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:32.660
Well I think Oliver gave the-the neurotypical
world insight and how different kinds of minds

00:49:32.660 --> 00:49:34.330
perceive the world.

00:49:34.330 --> 00:49:36.950
He was a great observer.

00:49:36.950 --> 00:49:41.750
Although some people in the medical community
that criticized them because what he was doing

00:49:41.750 --> 00:49:46.210
is observation, but observation is part of
science.

00:49:46.210 --> 00:49:50.660
I used to fight with my major professor when
I was getting my Ph.D. all the time he'd say,

00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:54.550
"Well you got to do a controlled experiment,
otherwise it's not science."

00:49:54.550 --> 00:49:57.310
And I'd go, "OK what's astronomy.?"

00:49:57.310 --> 00:49:59.690
But in order to have a hypothesis.

00:49:59.690 --> 00:50:04.910
you've got to observe first and Oliver was
a great observer.

00:50:04.910 --> 00:50:09.360
He definitely did not- he definitely did not
write like a scientist.

00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:14.940
One of the big fights he got in science is
some hardcore scientists say, "Well unless

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:19.150
you've got a control group an experimental
group, it's not science."

00:50:19.150 --> 00:50:25.080
Oliver was an observer an observation is a
very important part of science.

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:30.270
There's no way that you're going to have a
hypothesis until you have an observation to

00:50:30.270 --> 00:50:34.320
make the hypothesis so you do the other controlled
experiments.

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:38.770
The observers actually are the leaders.

00:50:38.770 --> 00:50:42.990
Well I think this book suggests going- you
know, a lot of young doctors are going to

00:50:42.990 --> 00:50:43.990
read us books.

00:50:43.990 --> 00:50:48.600
I mean a lot of family members read his books.

00:50:48.600 --> 00:50:53.700
I'm- people, parents of autistic children
have read the article did about me.

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:59.310
You know, he's a- he's going to live on in
his writings.

00:50:59.310 --> 00:51:03.240
And he wrote down everything before he died.

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:10.470
He was writing right up until he died.

00:51:10.470 --> 00:51:18.290
Hi I'm Connie Tomaino and the friend of Oliver's
for over 35 years.

00:51:18.290 --> 00:51:20.550
How does music affect the brain?

00:51:20.550 --> 00:51:25.560
That was actually the question that started
our friendship so many years ago.

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:31.390
In 1998, I was working in a small nursing
home with people with end stages of Alzheimer's

00:51:31.390 --> 00:51:33.480
disease and dementia.

00:51:33.480 --> 00:51:39.130
People I was told had no recognition of themselves
or anything around them.

00:51:39.130 --> 00:51:43.180
For all intents and purposes, they had no
brains at all.

00:51:43.180 --> 00:51:45.820
At least that's what the doctors would tell
me.

00:51:45.820 --> 00:51:51.980
And yet each one of those individuals, I could
find this song, a piece of music that could

00:51:51.980 --> 00:51:56.990
bring them to life, to make a connection,
to have them interact with me and the world

00:51:56.990 --> 00:51:58.000
around them.

00:51:58.000 --> 00:52:03.770
In 1980, I was offered the full time position
at Beth Abraham Hospital and within the first

00:52:03.770 --> 00:52:09.580
week there, I received the manila envelope
in- into office mail addressed to the music

00:52:09.580 --> 00:52:15.810
therapist and inside that envelope was a torn
piece of loose leaf paper with words scribbled

00:52:15.810 --> 00:52:18.550
on it in magic marker.

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:21.460
Every sickness is a musical problem.

00:52:21.460 --> 00:52:24.910
Every cure, a musical solution.

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:26.590
No volus.

00:52:26.590 --> 00:52:27.590
Welcome.

00:52:27.590 --> 00:52:28.590
Ollie.

00:52:28.590 --> 00:52:33.470
I soon discovered that Ollie was the neurologist's
the staff neurologist at Beth Abraham and

00:52:33.470 --> 00:52:38.120
of course started stalking his- his office
just for the chance to meet him in person.

00:52:38.120 --> 00:52:43.890
One day I saw a patient that I had started
working with, a woman who was rigid, pretty

00:52:43.890 --> 00:52:47.120
much non-responsive, nonverbal, at least.

00:52:47.120 --> 00:52:51.910
We were told she was non-verbal, but she did
respond to gospel music.

00:52:51.910 --> 00:52:56.730
And when I sang those songs, not only did
she sing along, but she became animated and

00:52:56.730 --> 00:52:58.310
started talking.

00:52:58.310 --> 00:53:02.320
So I told Oliver I said, "Dr. Sacks, you have
to see her sing.

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:03.530
You have to see her speak."

00:53:03.530 --> 00:53:05.510
And he says, "Come show me come show me."

00:53:05.510 --> 00:53:10.940
And I sat next to him and he gently held her
hand and I sang and she came to life and he

00:53:10.940 --> 00:53:12.210
was amazed.

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:13.210
How is this possible?

00:53:13.210 --> 00:53:15.390
So immediately he reached over to his desk.

00:53:15.390 --> 00:53:18.540
He had a stack of EEG's and he pulled out
one.

00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:19.720
Then he says, "Look at this.

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:21.890
Look at this irregularly EEG's.

00:53:21.890 --> 00:53:22.890
This is Ed.

00:53:22.890 --> 00:53:23.890
He has Parkinson's."

00:53:23.890 --> 00:53:25.900
He says, "Look at this.

00:53:25.900 --> 00:53:26.930
This is Ed.

00:53:26.930 --> 00:53:29.000
This is Ed thinking about music.

00:53:29.000 --> 00:53:31.810
Look how beautiful the EEG is.

00:53:31.810 --> 00:53:35.790
"From then on, I started getting notes into
all of his medical referrals.

00:53:35.790 --> 00:53:41.310
For me to look at how the person responded
when I played.

00:53:41.310 --> 00:53:42.760
Did they tap their foot?

00:53:42.760 --> 00:53:43.760
Could they sing?

00:53:43.760 --> 00:53:44.890
Could they initiate a word?

00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:46.510
What did it look like?

00:53:46.510 --> 00:53:50.690
Again I started to observe every single thing
I did to the music.

00:53:50.690 --> 00:53:56.510
What was it about music that enabled these
people to come to life, to become whole again

00:53:56.510 --> 00:53:59.530
and he with I would try to talk as much as
possible.

00:53:59.530 --> 00:54:01.880
but that work was really difficult.

00:54:01.880 --> 00:54:06.040
He lived on City island, he had just bought
the home back then, and I live very close

00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:07.040
by.

00:54:07.040 --> 00:54:11.721
So he started to invite me over in the evening
for dinner just to talk with whether we know

00:54:11.721 --> 00:54:12.721
about music.

00:54:12.721 --> 00:54:13.721
What does it look like?

00:54:13.721 --> 00:54:15.640
How did the patients respond?

00:54:15.640 --> 00:54:20.350
And for every question I had, and I was loaded
with them, he would pull a book from one of

00:54:20.350 --> 00:54:21.820
his shelves.

00:54:21.820 --> 00:54:22.870
Henry Head.

00:54:22.870 --> 00:54:24.130
Hughlings Jackson.

00:54:24.130 --> 00:54:29.290
Every single question I had, he had a quote
from some book that he had the first edition

00:54:29.290 --> 00:54:32.780
of.

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:35.690
We tried- We knew that there was something
about music in the brain.

00:54:35.690 --> 00:54:41.670
We knew that there was some networks elements
that arouse fundamental function in these

00:54:41.670 --> 00:54:44.320
individuals who seemed otherwise unreachable.

00:54:44.320 --> 00:54:49.010
And in the early 80s, we actually try to meet
with scientists to see if we can encourage

00:54:49.010 --> 00:54:50.710
them to study music in the brain.

00:54:50.710 --> 00:54:55.690
But they said the field of neurosciences is
way too new and music is so complex.

00:54:55.690 --> 00:54:57.970
How can we possibly study that?

00:54:57.970 --> 00:55:03.010
But Oliver started writing these case studies
and he started pulling them out in the London

00:55:03.010 --> 00:55:07.780
Review of Books in the New York Review of
Books and the literary world as you know started

00:55:07.780 --> 00:55:09.380
to take notice.

00:55:09.380 --> 00:55:14.420
And I remember a time when Susan Sontag invited
him to speak at Cooper Union then.

00:55:14.420 --> 00:55:20.900
Oliver wasn't yet ready for prime time public
speaker and he said Connie please come along

00:55:20.900 --> 00:55:22.130
with me, please come along with me.

00:55:22.130 --> 00:55:26.910
And we- we went there and I spent an hour
before him of course, this was pre Kate days

00:55:26.910 --> 00:55:33.220
so he would drag me along on all these talks
and he said, "Please, let's go over the patients.

00:55:33.220 --> 00:55:34.390
What do they look like?

00:55:34.390 --> 00:55:35.390
What should I say?

00:55:35.390 --> 00:55:36.390
What- what's going on?

00:55:36.390 --> 00:55:39.460
And of course he wrote all of his notes on
his arm.

00:55:39.460 --> 00:55:46.380
And my only, my only job he told me, was to
sit in the front so he could see me and any

00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:49.710
time he started to stammer just go like this.

00:55:49.710 --> 00:55:53.640
So he would look at his arm and get back on
track.

00:55:53.640 --> 00:55:59.810
His work, the movies started to gain much
recognition and though the administration

00:55:59.810 --> 00:56:03.430
started to recognize the importance of the
work that we were trying to do.

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:09.440
To understand how to reach people who seemed
otherwise unreachable and for whom many medical

00:56:09.440 --> 00:56:15.930
science and the medical community had deemed
at the end stages of life.

00:56:15.930 --> 00:56:20.670
Yet these people were still very much full
with life and we could animate them and bring

00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:23.070
them into the present.

00:56:23.070 --> 00:56:30.030
So the administration helped found the Institute
for Music and Neurologic Function in 1995

00:56:30.030 --> 00:56:36.350
as a bridge between the worlds of neuroscience
and clinical music therapy to form dialogues

00:56:36.350 --> 00:56:42.790
across these disciplines to not only advance
the scientific knowledge about how music affects

00:56:42.790 --> 00:56:49.010
human function, but also to help clinical
music therapists apply it in more efficacious

00:56:49.010 --> 00:56:50.010
ways.

00:56:50.010 --> 00:56:56.640
There were no words that I can say right now
that can tell you the depths of my gratitude

00:56:56.640 --> 00:56:58.810
and love for Oliver.

00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:04.250
Not only for what he has given me as a clinician
and in our friendship, but what he has given

00:57:04.250 --> 00:57:08.670
the world as we've heard about bringing the
humanness back to the individuals that we

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:09.960
take care of.

00:57:09.960 --> 00:57:14.810
To recognizing the potential in- in those
people as well.

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:19.060
And also bringing the face and knowledge about
the field of music therapy and the importance

00:57:19.060 --> 00:57:23.070
of that work to help so many people.

00:57:23.070 --> 00:57:31.190
And it's very appropriate and they can demonstrate,
or you'll get the chance to actually witness

00:57:31.190 --> 00:57:33.260
the importance of music.

00:57:33.260 --> 00:57:36.990
Especially how music affects the brain.

00:57:36.990 --> 00:57:45.440
As I welcome and you hear and listen to Eric
Jordan, Metropolitan Opera singer, who's also

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:52.520
a stroke survivor who regained his speech
and the ability to talk through the extraordinary

00:57:52.520 --> 00:57:54.710
power of music.

00:57:54.710 --> 00:57:58.010
Thank you.

00:57:58.010 --> 00:58:01.310
Yeah, absolutely.

00:58:01.310 --> 00:58:11.300
Yes I'm a stroke survivor and a friend of
mine lent me the book called The Man Who Mistook

00:58:11.300 --> 00:58:17.690
His Wife For A Hat and thought myself- myself
should be an opera.

00:58:17.690 --> 00:58:19.560
And it is.

00:58:19.560 --> 00:58:30.750
It's a chamber opera and my character Dr.
P, suffers from visual agnosia.

00:58:30.750 --> 00:58:34.460
He has hallucinations without needing drugs.

00:58:34.460 --> 00:58:39.960
And this is a dream come true.

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:45.060
Thank you Connie for reaching out to me to
sing for everybody here.

00:58:45.060 --> 00:58:48.490
You are all observers, right?

00:58:48.490 --> 00:58:55.010
And I've had a quick story to share before
I sing.

00:58:55.010 --> 00:59:02.480
In a deli somewhere by the net the Met, I
couldn't speak my order.

00:59:02.480 --> 00:59:05.350
So I sang it.

00:59:05.350 --> 00:59:12.000
I said, "I'd like a whole wheat footlong with
roast beef with mustard sauce with lettuce

00:59:12.000 --> 00:59:15.000
and tomatoes please."

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:21.360
And the dude across the counter is like, "Who's
wackado is singing- is singing- singing his

00:59:21.360 --> 00:59:22.360
order.

00:59:22.360 --> 00:59:26.510
Okay we'll give this guy some horseradish
please."

00:59:26.510 --> 00:59:28.180
And this is New York City, right?

00:59:28.180 --> 00:59:31.200
So wackado central.

00:59:31.200 --> 00:59:37.260
In a good way.

00:59:37.260 --> 01:00:50.930
So before I keep on jibber jabbering, let's
make some music.

01:00:50.930 --> 01:01:53.460
(Singing)
I'm Fay Wright and 

01:01:53.460 --> 01:02:33.160
I had 

01:02:33.160 --> 01:02:37.940
the great pleasure to be Oliver’s piano
teacher for about seven years.

01:02:37.940 --> 01:02:46.702
When we met, Oliver was about 75 and he had
had his last piano lesson when he was 12.He

01:02:46.702 --> 01:02:54.010
had adored his childhood teacher and was really
hesitant even after all those decades to study

01:02:54.010 --> 01:02:55.160
with anyone else.

01:02:55.160 --> 01:02:59.520
This is just an example of the depth of his
loyalty to the people in his life.

01:02:59.520 --> 01:03:01.880
And I was soon to feel that loyalty myself.

01:03:01.880 --> 01:03:06.340
We became fast friends very quickly and he
was a lot of fun.

01:03:06.340 --> 01:03:07.630
His playing style was pretty unique.

01:03:07.630 --> 01:03:13.660
When I first met him, he would raise his arms
high and proceed to bounce off the keys as

01:03:13.660 --> 01:03:15.530
though they were on fire.

01:03:15.530 --> 01:03:18.380
He would clamp the pedal down and play everything
staccato and forte.

01:03:18.380 --> 01:03:24.460
If he ran out of fingers, he would simply
flip his hand over in a sort of Chico Marx

01:03:24.460 --> 01:03:27.450
maneuver and use his index finger.

01:03:27.450 --> 01:03:33.570
He played with a lot of elan.

01:03:33.570 --> 01:03:39.110
He was not crazy about music after 1920 or
so.

01:03:39.110 --> 01:03:44.210
He was very fond of Debussy, but that was
about as modern as he was willing to get.

01:03:44.210 --> 01:03:47.990
I convinced him to play some Bartók Romanian
dances.

01:03:47.990 --> 01:03:51.680
I think that was partly because he'd heard
his father playing them.

01:03:51.680 --> 01:03:57.720
He had a very personal association with many
pieces of music and he always expressed interest

01:03:57.720 --> 01:04:03.920
in Liszt, because he remembered his brother
David playing Liszt.

01:04:03.920 --> 01:04:14.020
I was always amazed at his perseverance with
the piano in face of great physical obstacles.

01:04:14.020 --> 01:04:18.770
His back was in terrible pain, he had bad
insomnia.

01:04:18.770 --> 01:04:25.619
And h is thumbs were distorted by arthritis
and he was blind in one eye.

01:04:25.619 --> 01:04:31.910
So he had to have many, many cushions sitting
on the bench in order to be comfortable.

01:04:31.910 --> 01:04:37.590
And he had to enlarge every page of his music
for the pieces that we worked on.

01:04:37.590 --> 01:04:41.970
Even then, he often had to resort to a magnifying
glass that he kept on the side of the piano

01:04:41.970 --> 01:04:44.100
to peer at the score.

01:04:44.100 --> 01:04:46.410
So it was very difficult for him.

01:04:46.410 --> 01:04:58.790
But he didn't lead and he didn't let that
slow him down at all.

01:04:58.790 --> 01:05:04.240
I attribute my mother with being my first
music teacher.

01:05:04.240 --> 01:05:10.650
She grew up in a farm in Pennsylvania and
when she was a teenager, she was the rehearsal

01:05:10.650 --> 01:05:16.950
pianist at the Methodist Church in the town
of Boothwyn.

01:05:16.950 --> 01:05:24.330
My parents always felt that it was important
to be surrounded by music.

01:05:24.330 --> 01:05:26.270
We all studied instruments.

01:05:26.270 --> 01:05:30.740
We all played instruments by being surrounded
with music.

01:05:30.740 --> 01:05:36.290
It helped us become whole people.

01:05:36.290 --> 01:05:43.540
My mother used to gather us around the piano
and she'd play from the Methodist hymnal and

01:05:43.540 --> 01:05:48.660
my brothers and sister and I would- would
sing from the Methodist hymnal and we would

01:05:48.660 --> 01:05:51.860
be taught harmonies at a very early age.

01:05:51.860 --> 01:05:56.090
My mother was always the sharpest knife in
the drawer.

01:05:56.090 --> 01:06:03.520
She was very funny, very smart, but she began
to lose her memory when my father passed away

01:06:03.520 --> 01:06:06.490
18 years ago.

01:06:06.490 --> 01:06:11.619
And one of the beautiful things about living
with someone with dementia, is that it's all

01:06:11.619 --> 01:06:16.240
about the present moment the past is history.

01:06:16.240 --> 01:06:20.840
The future has no frame of reference, so it's
really what's in front of you.

01:06:20.840 --> 01:06:23.810
The clouds passing in the sky.

01:06:23.810 --> 01:06:29.770
The softness of a cat, a beautiful sunset.

01:06:29.770 --> 01:06:36.910
My mother three years ago when she had to
have 24 hour care and could no longer live

01:06:36.910 --> 01:06:43.030
with us, moved to the actor's home in Englewood
New Jersey.

01:06:43.030 --> 01:06:49.690
And when I go and visit her, sometimes I can
get her to sit at the piano and she'll play

01:06:49.690 --> 01:06:51.300
from the Methodist hymnal.

01:06:51.300 --> 01:06:58.160
The very same Methodist hymnal that she taught
me to sing from as a child.

01:06:58.160 --> 01:07:09.730
And when she plays the piano and we sing together
in harmony, suddenly I'm a child again.

01:07:09.730 --> 01:07:14.330
And she's a young mother, and we're in the
moment.

01:07:14.330 --> 01:07:19.290
And in the moment, it becomes transcendent.

01:07:19.290 --> 01:07:59.020
And I have a glimpse 
of 

01:07:59.020 --> 01:10:00.270
that which is eternal.

01:10:00.270 --> 01:10:56.340
A glimpse of forever.

01:10:56.340 --> 01:11:23.420
(Singing)
So my name is Slava Santoriello.

01:11:23.420 --> 01:11:28.030
And I know Oliver for many, many years.

01:11:28.030 --> 01:11:34.630
And actually my first experience with him
was a little bit odd and funny at the same

01:11:34.630 --> 01:11:36.130
time.

01:11:36.130 --> 01:11:37.270
He approached me in the pool.

01:11:37.270 --> 01:11:38.520
He was waiting on the side.

01:11:38.520 --> 01:11:43.230
I was teaching, and he was waiting on the
side.

01:11:43.230 --> 01:11:49.440
And when I stop, he approached me and he said,
"I want you to be my coach."

01:11:49.440 --> 01:11:53.790
So my first response was Why me?

01:11:53.790 --> 01:11:56.980
And he said, "Because you're the best."

01:11:56.980 --> 01:11:58.340
OK.

01:11:58.340 --> 01:12:01.500
The funny thing.

01:12:01.500 --> 01:12:09.190
Two months later, he done absolutely- he refuse
to do anything I told him to do.

01:12:09.190 --> 01:12:15.650
So to get to the point when I said," Oliver,
if you think I'm the best why you're not doing

01:12:15.650 --> 01:12:17.290
anything.

01:12:17.290 --> 01:12:22.020
And if you know everything, why you need me?"

01:12:22.020 --> 01:12:26.590
It's not just I teach him how to swim.

01:12:26.590 --> 01:12:32.470
He actually teach me how to be a better coach,
how to be a better instructor.

01:12:32.470 --> 01:12:37.770
And I find out- because I thought at the beginning
that he's very stubborn, doesn't want to learn

01:12:37.770 --> 01:12:39.870
anything new.

01:12:39.870 --> 01:12:45.110
And that was so wrong.

01:12:45.110 --> 01:12:46.110
Really.

01:12:46.110 --> 01:12:49.650
I think he's the most open person I ever met.

01:12:49.650 --> 01:12:55.750
And he's really eager to learn something new.

01:12:55.750 --> 01:13:00.340
And with marvelous, wonderful job together.

01:13:00.340 --> 01:13:07.440
But every time, literally every single time
when he will come for practicing, he'll be

01:13:07.440 --> 01:13:10.150
excited.

01:13:10.150 --> 01:13:13.920
Excited that he will be in the water, that
he will do again something.

01:13:13.920 --> 01:13:16.260
And he will feel very, very free.

01:13:16.260 --> 01:13:18.880
And it's a very rare thing.

01:13:18.880 --> 01:13:30.840
Yes he definitely was in love with-with water.

01:13:30.840 --> 01:13:35.760
My name is Ian Sample and I was Oliver's personal
trainer.

01:13:35.760 --> 01:13:37.680
People say like what was Oliver like?

01:13:37.680 --> 01:13:41.520
And I say, he's like a Renaissance man where
you don't really see too many people like

01:13:41.520 --> 01:13:42.520
that.

01:13:42.520 --> 01:13:48.220
You don't see too many people that are a marathon
swimmer, that could squat 600 pounds, which

01:13:48.220 --> 01:13:49.730
is still super impressive.

01:13:49.730 --> 01:13:53.810
I don't know if anyone knows how much weight
that really is but it's insane.

01:13:53.810 --> 01:13:57.510
And also was a scientist and a published writer
and author.

01:13:57.510 --> 01:13:59.130
He's just such an amazing person.

01:13:59.130 --> 01:14:04.180
He could look at an element, whether it's
a rock, whether it's I turn 33 or 32 and he

01:14:04.180 --> 01:14:07.730
gives me the element like gallium or something
like that.

01:14:07.730 --> 01:14:13.060
He just looked at something that I think is
really simple with such big fascination and

01:14:13.060 --> 01:14:15.080
such big eyes.

01:14:15.080 --> 01:14:19.520
I mean he's looked at these things for over
probably 70 years and he still is so fascinated

01:14:19.520 --> 01:14:24.360
by that.

01:14:24.360 --> 01:14:28.560
Why is science important?

01:14:28.560 --> 01:14:31.390
Why is science important?

01:14:31.390 --> 01:14:37.100
Because it gives one a picture of the world,
I think, which one can get in any other way.

01:14:37.100 --> 01:14:41.140
Now it's not the only picture.

01:14:41.140 --> 01:14:42.710
And it mustn't be the only picture.

01:14:42.710 --> 01:14:47.660
I mean I think one-one needs to sort of read
the poets, and listen to music, and maybe

01:14:47.660 --> 01:14:49.370
be religious.

01:14:49.370 --> 01:14:50.870
When one needs every sort of picture.

01:14:50.870 --> 01:14:56.590
But I think one has to have the scientific
picture as well and one always did.

01:14:56.590 --> 01:15:02.460
And even if there are no formal laws, without
science, people would never have made fire,

01:15:02.460 --> 01:15:07.250
would never have made clothes, would never
build houses, would never discovered agriculture.

01:15:07.250 --> 01:15:13.340
Civilization is partly dependent on science.

01:15:13.340 --> 01:15:14.920
My name is Laura Snider.

01:15:14.920 --> 01:15:19.000
I'm a writer and I'm a philosophy professor
at St. John's University.

01:15:19.000 --> 01:15:26.380
I later found that Oliver’s favorite philosopher
was David Hume, who, the 18th century philosopher

01:15:26.380 --> 01:15:33.200
David Hume, who said that he wanted to be
essentially the Newton of the moral realm.

01:15:33.200 --> 01:15:39.270
David Hume wanted to take the study of human
nature and make it as important as the study

01:15:39.270 --> 01:15:45.150
of physics was in that period of time, which
was so soon after Isaac Newton had revolutionized

01:15:45.150 --> 01:15:46.150
physics.

01:15:46.150 --> 01:15:49.250
And I always felt that Oliver’s work was
like that.

01:15:49.250 --> 01:15:56.630
He wanted to elevate the study of the human
elements of neurological disease.

01:15:56.630 --> 01:16:01.940
He wanted to look at the human dimension and
make that a subject of interest to the physician

01:16:01.940 --> 01:16:05.820
as much as the physical dimension of the disease.

01:16:05.820 --> 01:16:12.630
And to me, that's a very generous way of looking
at neurological illness.

01:16:12.630 --> 01:16:17.450
I sometimes felt that I had left England in
an underhand way.

01:16:17.450 --> 01:16:20.210
I'd had the best of English educations.

01:16:20.210 --> 01:16:24.110
I'd absorbed the best of English diction and
prose.

01:16:24.110 --> 01:16:27.239
The habits and traditions of a thousand years.

01:16:27.239 --> 01:16:32.710
And here I was taking this precious mental
cargo, everything that had been invested in

01:16:32.710 --> 01:16:37.770
me, out of the country without so much as
a thank you or goodbye.

01:16:37.770 --> 01:16:43.980
I did not seek American citizenship and was
happy to have a green card to be a counted

01:16:43.980 --> 01:16:46.620
a resident alien.

01:16:46.620 --> 01:16:51.470
This accorded with how I felt, at least for
much of the time.

01:16:51.470 --> 01:16:55.860
A friendly observant alien noting everything
around me.

01:16:55.860 --> 01:17:02.450
But without civic responsibilities, such as
voting or jury duty or need to affiliate myself

01:17:02.450 --> 01:17:11.620
with a country's policies or politics, I often
felt as Temple Grandin said of herself, that

01:17:11.620 --> 01:17:15.130
I was an anthropologist on Mars.

01:17:15.130 --> 01:17:23.000
And then in June of 2008, to my surprise,
I heard that my name was on the Queen's Birthday

01:17:23.000 --> 01:17:24.000
honors list.

01:17:24.000 --> 01:17:28.420
That I was to become a commander of the British
Empire.

01:17:28.420 --> 01:17:30.560
The term "commander" tickled me.

01:17:30.560 --> 01:17:37.870
I could not imagine myself as a commander
on the bridge of a destroyer or a battleship.

01:17:37.870 --> 01:17:42.720
But I was curiously, and rather deeply moved
by the honor.

01:17:42.720 --> 01:17:48.320
Though I am not given to formal clothes or
other formalities, normally my clothes are

01:17:48.320 --> 01:17:49.710
sloppy and decrepit.

01:17:49.710 --> 01:17:52.950
And I have only one suit.

01:17:52.950 --> 01:17:56.310
I enjoy the formalities of Buckingham Palace.

01:17:56.310 --> 01:18:04.400
Being instructed how to bow, how to walk backwards
before the Queen, how to await her and for

01:18:04.400 --> 01:18:05.610
addressing one.

01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:11.400
The royal person was not to be touched or
spoken to unbidden.

01:18:11.400 --> 01:18:14.480
I was half afraid that I would do something
awful.

01:18:14.480 --> 01:18:18.920
Like faint or fart right in front of the Queen.

01:18:18.920 --> 01:18:20.840
But all went well.

01:18:20.840 --> 01:18:25.750
During the ceremony, I was very impressed
by the Queen's stamina.

01:18:25.750 --> 01:18:31.510
By the time I was called up, she'd been standing
erect without support for more than two hours.

01:18:31.510 --> 01:18:35.360
Two of 200 honorees that day.

01:18:35.360 --> 01:18:40.330
She spoke to me briefly but warmly asked me
what I was working on.

01:18:40.330 --> 01:18:47.450
I had the feeling of a very decent friendly
person with a sense of humor.

01:18:47.450 --> 01:18:57.040
It was as if she and England were saying,
"You have done useful honorable work.

01:18:57.040 --> 01:19:01.700
Come home.

01:19:01.700 --> 01:19:08.680
All is forgiven."

01:19:08.680 --> 01:19:14.440
They called me "Inky" as a boy.

01:19:14.440 --> 01:19:20.570
And I still seem to get as ink stained as
I did 70 years ago.

01:19:20.570 --> 01:19:24.560
I started keeping journals when I was 14.

01:19:24.560 --> 01:19:27.840
And at last count, had nearly a thousand.

01:19:27.840 --> 01:19:33.860
They come in all shapes and sizes, from little
pocket ones, which I carry around with me,

01:19:33.860 --> 01:19:36.690
to enormous tomes.

01:19:36.690 --> 01:19:43.040
I always keep a notebook by my bedside for
dreams as well as nighttime thoughts.

01:19:43.040 --> 01:19:49.510
And I try to have one by the swimming pool,
or the lake side, or the seashore.

01:19:49.510 --> 01:19:55.150
Swimming too, is very productive of thoughts,
which I must write, especially if they present

01:19:55.150 --> 01:20:02.040
themselves as they sometimes do in the form
of whole sentences or paragraphs.

01:20:02.040 --> 01:20:08.611
While writing my next book, I drew heavily
on the detailed journals I had kept as a patient

01:20:08.611 --> 01:20:10.640
in 1974.

01:20:10.640 --> 01:20:17.030
My journal too, relied on my handwritten note
books.

01:20:17.030 --> 01:20:23.840
But for the most part, I rarely look at the
journals I've kept for the greater part of

01:20:23.840 --> 01:20:24.840
a lifetime.

01:20:24.840 --> 01:20:29.030
The act of writing is itself enough.

01:20:29.030 --> 01:20:32.370
It serves to clarify my thoughts and feelings.

01:20:32.370 --> 01:20:38.840
The act of writing is an integral part of
my mental life.

01:20:38.840 --> 01:20:43.340
Ideas emerge are shaped in the act of writing.

01:20:43.340 --> 01:20:46.170
My journals are not written for others.

01:20:46.170 --> 01:20:50.000
Nor do I usually look at them myself.

01:20:50.000 --> 01:21:00.720
But they are a special, indispensable form
of talking to myself.

01:21:00.720 --> 01:21:04.200
I am a storyteller, for better and for worse.

01:21:04.200 --> 01:21:07.300
I suspect the feeling for stories, for narrative,
is a universal human disposition.

01:21:07.300 --> 01:21:14.520
Going without powers of language, consciousness
of self, and autobiographical memory.

01:21:14.520 --> 01:21:22.120
The act of writing when it goes well, gives
me a pleasure or joy unlike any other.

01:21:22.120 --> 01:21:25.020
It takes me to another place.

01:21:25.020 --> 01:21:32.980
Whatever my subject where I am totally absorbed
and oblivious to distracting thoughts, worries,

01:21:32.980 --> 01:21:37.850
preoccupations, or indeed, the passage of
time.

01:21:37.850 --> 01:21:46.340
In these rare heavenly states, in these rare
heavenly states of mind, I may write nonstop

01:21:46.340 --> 01:21:50.270
until I can no longer see the paper.

01:21:50.270 --> 01:21:53.160
Only then do I realize that evening has come.

01:21:53.160 --> 01:21:55.210
And that I have been writing all day.

01:21:55.210 --> 01:22:00.240
Over a lifetime, I've written millions of
words.

01:22:00.240 --> 01:22:08.610
But the act of writing seems as fresh and
as much fun as when I started it nearly 70

01:22:08.610 --> 01:22:13.350
years ago.

01:22:13.350 --> 01:22:59.890
Needless to say, I'm an incredible admirer
of all of his accomplishments.

01:22:59.890 --> 01:23:04.610
But- but that's not been an obstacle in our
friendship.

01:23:04.610 --> 01:23:12.219
You know, I haven't you know people think
of Oliver Sacks, Oliver Sacks, Oliver Sacks

01:23:12.219 --> 01:23:14.120
is my friend.

01:23:14.120 --> 01:23:22.150
I know very-very well who's been in my house
numerable times, knows all my kids, who knows

01:23:22.150 --> 01:23:25.750
my husband, and I know about his family.

01:23:25.750 --> 01:23:29.540
We've talked about all sorts of things about
his family.

01:23:29.540 --> 01:23:33.570
I mean he's my friend.

01:23:33.570 --> 01:23:45.910
In my heart, I think Oliver was literally
able to expand the reach of everybody's neighbor

01:23:45.910 --> 01:23:51.090
and to include people who had been excluded.

01:23:51.090 --> 01:23:55.739
And that's- you know, he was a complicated
guy.

01:23:55.739 --> 01:24:01.200
But that's an achievement.

01:24:01.200 --> 01:24:06.080
It was just such a pleasure to work with Oliver
via music.

01:24:06.080 --> 01:24:15.820
That we became such good friends through that
medium and I learned so much from him and

01:24:15.820 --> 01:24:18.230
I was very grateful for that time with him.

01:24:18.230 --> 01:24:25.170
Oh if I could talk with Oliver now or send
him a letter, I would say, "Let's go out and

01:24:25.170 --> 01:24:26.170
have some sushi.

01:24:26.170 --> 01:24:33.240
And I want to hear about your next book and
what you working on."

01:24:33.240 --> 01:24:35.050
He was a presence in my life for 20 years.

01:24:35.050 --> 01:24:36.550
I learned so much from him.

01:24:36.550 --> 01:24:40.420
I go back and read his books.

01:24:40.420 --> 01:24:42.370
Dozens of letters he wrote to me.

01:24:42.370 --> 01:24:48.050
We would change exchange books and articles
all the time.

01:24:48.050 --> 01:24:50.710
And I'm grateful for the example.

01:24:50.710 --> 01:24:55.670
I think I would tell them that I'm excited
to go to Mexico in three weeks.

01:24:55.670 --> 01:25:00.860
I'm going to go snorkeling and scuba diving
and I'm going to take the wet suit that he

01:25:00.860 --> 01:25:06.770
let me have and I'll take lots of pictures
for him.

01:25:06.770 --> 01:25:11.090
If I could say one thing to Oliver or write
him a letter, Oliver wherever you are.

01:25:11.090 --> 01:25:14.050
I would say Thank you.

01:25:14.050 --> 01:25:23.310
And I think I would also tell him that sitting
under these lights, being filmed, talking

01:25:23.310 --> 01:25:34.380
about him had an oddly calming effect on my
tics such that for the most part for most

01:25:34.380 --> 01:25:44.020
of the interview, I felt just like anybody
else.

01:25:44.020 --> 01:25:50.400
I just want to say that I love him.

01:25:50.400 --> 01:25:55.150
I love him not just as my students.

01:25:55.150 --> 01:26:01.540
I love him for who he is.

01:26:01.540 --> 01:26:04.920
And because it's not just me.

01:26:04.920 --> 01:26:10.220
I think he touched so many hearts in so many
ways.

01:26:10.220 --> 01:26:12.270
That's a wonderful ability.

01:26:12.270 --> 01:26:20.111
Thank you for teaching many a generation of
writer that it was important to look at the

01:26:20.111 --> 01:26:22.570
human dimension of science.

01:26:22.570 --> 01:26:26.480
And it was important to bring science to a
broader public, because that's the only way

01:26:26.480 --> 01:26:31.380
that people will become scientifically literate,
and be able to make the decisions that we

01:26:31.380 --> 01:26:34.250
need to make today.

01:26:34.250 --> 01:26:40.190
I would say that last message that I would
have written to Oliver would have just been

01:26:40.190 --> 01:26:41.300
thank you.

01:26:41.300 --> 01:26:47.680
And it's very- very rare to watch a man grow
closer and closer to death.

01:26:47.680 --> 01:26:54.220
And have his eyes get wider and wider open
as he moves towards that place.

01:26:54.220 --> 01:26:58.900
I have never seen anything like it.

01:26:58.900 --> 01:27:05.820
I would say Oliver was- was really one of
the best of the physicians I've run across,

01:27:05.820 --> 01:27:09.170
ever and in any institutions.

01:27:09.170 --> 01:27:16.320
Treated his patients respectfully, and was
able, from listening closely for pulling details

01:27:16.320 --> 01:27:21.260
of it with a patient had to say, and listening
to things that many people would consider

01:27:21.260 --> 01:27:31.410
to be extraneous, was able to propel the science
of medicine in great fashion in ways that

01:27:31.410 --> 01:27:35.340
one should try to replicate.

01:27:35.340 --> 01:27:40.080
My last exchange was actually with the computer
in my kitchen.

01:27:40.080 --> 01:27:41.080
Crying.

01:27:41.080 --> 01:27:44.860
Crying so hard I couldn't figure out how to
print it out.

01:27:44.860 --> 01:27:49.070
Reading the last piece that he wrote, I was
crying so hard.

01:27:49.070 --> 01:27:51.050
That's kind of what I feel is my last exchange.

01:27:51.050 --> 01:27:59.350
Reading the New York Times article on my computer
and I just put my head on the desk and crying.

01:27:59.350 --> 01:28:07.420
James Baldwin used to say that the poets,
by which he meant, the artist is the only

01:28:07.420 --> 01:28:12.150
person who knows what it's like for anybody
who comes to this planet to survive it.

01:28:12.150 --> 01:28:16.590
What it's like to die, to have somebody die,
what it's like to be glad.

01:28:16.590 --> 01:28:24.260
And in a way, Dr. Sacks was this kind of poet
who was able to get to the innermost universal

01:28:24.260 --> 01:28:29.660
human experience through the very personal
experience of his patients himself.

01:28:29.660 --> 01:29:51.260
And articulate what that's like, what it's
like to be here, what it's like to live and

01:29:51.260 --> 01:31:00.670
why.

01:31:00.670 --> 01:33:19.469
(Piano performance)

